WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.929 --> 00:00:08.551
I go to school in the world's biggest classroom.

2
00:00:09.272 --> 00:00:15.494
My classroom is more than double the size of the United Kingdom, more than one and a half

3
00:00:15.654 --> 00:00:17.555
million square kilometers.

4
00:00:18.515 --> 00:00:22.557
I only see my teacher and classmates a few times a year!

5
00:00:22.577 --> 00:00:26.078
I am a student at the School of the Air.

6
00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:33.997
The School of the Air is in Australia, and it teaches around one thousand students nationwide.

7
00:00:35.057 --> 00:00:42.580
Many students, like me, live in isolated places in the vast area of Australia known as the outback.

8
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:50.062
The outback is the huge desert-like area in the middle of Australia, away from its coasts.

9
00:00:50.762 --> 00:00:52.602
Very few people live here.

10
00:00:53.342 --> 00:01:00.485
My nearest classmate is a four-hour drive away, and the nearest town is a six-hour drive.

11
00:01:02.307 --> 00:01:07.171
I live at Pine Creek Cattle Station, Northern Territory, Australia.

12
00:01:07.872 --> 00:01:14.257
Outback kids are different from city kids— I am 12 years old, but I ride a horse, work

13
00:01:14.337 --> 00:01:16.899
on a farm, and drive a motorcycle.

14
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.501
I don't have a license though!

15
00:01:20.642 --> 00:01:26.147
When I am 13, I will leave home and go to a boarding school in Darwin.

16
00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:30.190
Until then I will study at the School of the Air.

17
00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:31.931
How do I study?

18
00:01:32.472 --> 00:01:37.556
The word "air" is a clue—the School of the Air uses the radio.

19
00:01:39.918 --> 00:01:43.701
Monday to Friday I turn on the radio at eight o'clock.

20
00:01:44.241 --> 00:01:45.963
First up is the assembly.

21
00:01:46.483 --> 00:01:51.627
During assembly we sing the national anthem and the school song.

22
00:01:52.148 --> 00:01:59.129
Then we listen to other students do something special, like play the flute, read a poem they've written,

23
00:01:59.629 --> 00:02:00.809
or tell some jokes.

24
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.292
After the assembly there is lesson time for two hours.

25
00:02:06.992 --> 00:02:12.515
Mom or Dad sits next to me during lessons and helps me with my homework.

26
00:02:13.215 --> 00:02:16.076
After lunch there are extra activities.

27
00:02:16.876 --> 00:02:18.037
I am in the choir.

28
00:02:18.637 --> 00:02:24.900
At first it's strange singing into a radio microphone by yourself, but you get used to it!

29
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.824
On Wednesday I learn the flute over the radio, but Friday is my favorite day.

30
00:02:33.485 --> 00:02:39.249
After class is finished, I have a Girl Guides meeting—also on the radio!

31
00:02:39.889 --> 00:02:41.810
We are the Guides of the Air.

32
00:02:44.352 --> 00:02:47.214
But how do I study over the radio?

33
00:02:47.794 --> 00:02:54.499
First, I use a two-way radio, which means I can talk and listen to my teacher and the

34
00:02:54.579 --> 00:02:55.540
other students.

35
00:02:56.344 --> 00:03:04.048
Second, I use the Internet to email my teacher and look at websites for information and research.

36
00:03:06.089 --> 00:03:12.172
Third, every two weeks my teacher sends me books and homework through the mail.

37
00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:19.235
Fourth, I attend a school camp a few times a year, where I meet my teacher and my

38
00:03:19.295 --> 00:03:22.400
classmates face-to-face!

39
00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:27.725
Finally, my teacher comes and stays at my house for one week a year.

40
00:03:29.366 --> 00:03:33.309
Fifty years ago the School of the Air was very different.

41
00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:40.996
In those days the Flying Doctor was the only radio contact for people living in isolation.

42
00:03:41.576 --> 00:03:48.662
The Flying Doctor service is made up of doctors and nurses who fly to people in remote areas

43
00:03:48.942 --> 00:03:50.644
who need medical care.

44
00:03:51.392 --> 00:03:57.157
They give people medicine and medical advice or take them to the hospital.

45
00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:05.945
The Flying Doctor's radio, however, wasn't used just for medical news or advice.

46
00:04:06.726 --> 00:04:11.229
It also broadcast local news with its weekly galah session.

47
00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:19.853
A galah is an Australian bird that chatters a lot, and during the galah sessions, people talked to

48
00:04:19.933 --> 00:04:21.895
each other on the radio.

49
00:04:24.337 --> 00:04:27.580
Adelaide Miethke was a teacher in Alice Springs.

50
00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:36.007
She listened to the galah sessions on the radio and recognized that outback kids were very shy because

51
00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:39.430
they didn't get to meet other children very often.

52
00:04:40.071 --> 00:04:45.356
Adelaide Miethke suggested to the government that they start the School of the Air.

53
00:04:46.348 --> 00:04:54.135
The first broadcast was from Alice Springs in 1951, using the Flying Doctor's radio.

54
00:04:54.756 --> 00:04:59.720
Now there are more than 16 Schools of the Air around Australia.

55
00:05:00.421 --> 00:05:04.724
Mom said that back then, the radio didn't sound so clear.

56
00:05:04.744 --> 00:05:08.468
There were often breaks and strange noises.

57
00:05:09.188 --> 00:05:12.851
The teacher even had to power the radio with her feet!

58
00:05:14.992 --> 00:05:16.653
Of course things have changed.

59
00:05:17.034 --> 00:05:22.217
The Internet and satellites mean that radio lessons may disappear altogether.

60
00:05:22.237 --> 00:05:30.803
In the future I will probably use an electronic whiteboard and receive my lessons via satellite.

61
00:05:31.424 --> 00:05:35.447
In the meantime I enjoy my lessons over the airwaves.

62
00:05:36.187 --> 00:05:37.108
Over and out!
